# The conformance cases of UTS #46 for Unicode 17.0.0, run through
# `conform.failures` against `process`: every line of `IdnaTestV2.txt` of
# https://www.unicode.org/Public/17.0.0/idna/ (dated 2025-05-01, SHA-256
# beb5d0be20e89618...) but the 2 that hold a surrogate,
# which no `str` holds. Each case is ToUnicode, ToASCII and transitional
# ToASCII with every check on, as the file runs them, and UTS #46's X4_2 read
# as no error in ToUnicode, which reads no length.
#
# A case is `source;unicode;ascii;transitional;outcomes`, each text written as
# `conform.text_of` reads it: printable ASCII as itself but `^`, `"`, `\`,
# `{`, `}` and `;`, and every other character as `^`, its code point in
# hexadecimal, and `^`. An empty column is the column before it. The outcomes
# are one letter for each operation: `o` answers the text, `e` fails `invalid`.
#
# Made and not typed: a one-off program read the file on 2026-11-17, held
# every line to a reference of UTS #46 that used Python 3.15.2's NFC (6,391 of
# 6,391 lines agreeing), and wrote this module whole, marking `n`, a failure
# `unsupported`, the 2,532 operations on 844 lines whose normalization to NFC
# the package's quick check could not settle. On 2026-11-20, with the
# normalization `unicode/normalize`'s (the coherence review's D17), a second
# one-off program read the same file (SHA-256 checked), wrote every line
# again, held the 5,545 lines with no `n` to this module's own byte for byte,
# and replaced the 844 lines' outcomes by the file's: 694 `eee`, 102 `ooo`, 30
# `oee`, 18 `eeo`, their texts written. Nothing here is edited by hand. Only a
# test build reads the cases.

use url/idna/internal/conform

# How many cases the tests below run.
constant CASES: i64
    6389

## Tests

